pub(super) fn help(label: &str) -> Option<&'static str> {
    Some(match label {
        "Stock" => "The film the picture is taken on. Each stock brings the tone curve, colour sensitivity, sharpness and grain its maker measured. Tri-X ’56 is Tri-X as Kodak rated it in 1956, with coarser grain and a little less sharpness.",
        "Filter" => "A coloured filter over the lens. Film of this era is very sensitive to blue, so skies come out pale. Yellow, orange and red darken blue skies, each more than the last.",
        "Size" => "Width of the piece of film the picture fills, in mm. 36 is a full 35mm frame and 24 is that frame cropped a little. A smaller piece is enlarged more to make the same print, so its grain looks larger.",
        "Grid" => "How many cells across the film and print are simulated on before the result is enlarged to the photo's size. With Size, it sets how large the grain looks. A fixed grid gives the same picture from a small file and a large one. 0 simulates at the photo's own size.",
        "Development" => "How long the negative is developed. The app follows the curves the maker measured after each development time. Datasheet is the standard time. The 1952, 1956 and 1981 choices give the contrast Kodak recommended in those years.",
        "Minutes" => "Development time. Longer development gives a steeper curve, so more contrast, and slightly more grain. The range is the one the film's measurements cover.",
        "Auto" => "Sets the camera exposure from the photo, so its median brightness lands at the value on the right, from 0 (black) to 1 (white).",
        "Compensation" => "Stops of exposure added on top of Auto, or on top of the photo's own exposure when Auto is off. Each stop doubles the light reaching the film. More is brighter.",
        "Border" => "Shown when the photo has an even border round it, such as a print's paper, a mat or letterbox bars. Keep measures exposure and tones inside the border and leaves it in the picture. Strip does the same and crops the border away. Off doesn't look for one.",
        "Softness" => "Blur of the image on the film, in µm on the negative. It happens before development, so the grain stays sharp over a soft picture. 10 matches edge widths measured on the book's pages.",
        "Softness shape" => "Profile of the softness. 2 is an even Gaussian blur. Lower values keep a sharp core inside a long faint skirt, as real film does. 1.15 is fitted to 54 published film sharpness curves.",
        "Halation" => "A glow around bright areas, from light that passes through the film and reflects back off its base. 0.02 to 0.08 matches films with weak protection against it.",
        "Halation width" => "How far the halation glow spreads, in µm on the film.",
        "Amount" => "Grain strength relative to the film's measured graininess. Grain is simulated as single grains of silver that develop at random, not as a texture laid over the picture.",
        "In lights" => "How much grain stays in the light tones. The book's pages show less grain there than a straight print, because each copy and printing cleaned up the light end. 1 keeps it all.",
        "With development" => "How fast grain grows with longer development. 1.3 was measured on Double-X film. 0 keeps the grain fixed.",
        "Strength" => "Developer gets used up in dense parts of the negative and slows development right next to them. Along an edge the dark side gets darker and the light side lighter, much like gentle sharpening. Flat areas keep their tone. Too much shows as halos.",
        "Density" => "What the edge effect follows. 0 scales it with the tone difference across the edge alone. 1 follows Nelson's 1971 measurement, which makes fringes stronger in the light parts of the picture.",
        "Grade" => "Contrast of the photographic paper, from −1 (soft) to 5 (hard). A hard paper takes a narrow slice of the negative from white to black, so prints are punchy. A soft paper spreads the whole negative over its tones, so prints are gentler.",
        "Exposure" => "How long the enlarger lamp shines through the negative onto the paper, in stops. Paper darkens with light, so more is darker. Unlike Compensation, it leaves what the negative recorded alone.",
        "Grey density" => "How dark a metered mid grey prints before Exposure is applied. Density counts darkness in powers of ten: each 0.3 halves the light reflected, and 0.745 reflects 18%, a standard grey card.",
        "Enlarger blur" => "Softness from the enlarger, the projector that prints the negative onto paper, in µm on the negative. It comes after the grain, so it softens the grain too.",
        "House tones" => "Moves the picture's shadows and white point toward the range the book's pages share, measured from twelve of them. The book's makers copied every picture to this common look. 0 is a straight print and 1 matches the book.",
        "Finish" => "Where the process ends. Print is the darkroom print. Copy prints it in a book, photographs the page and prints it again, which is how the book's own pictures were made. Book prints it on the book's 1979 press, on uncoated paper.",
        "Copy blur" => "Softness of the copy photographed from the page, in thousandths of the picture's long edge. The book's scans measure 0.4 to 1.25 at page size. The default is lower, for viewing full screen.",
        "Copy contrast" => "Contrast added by the film the page was copied onto.",
        "Highlight drop" => "The palest tone lost at each printing, as the smallest highlight dots fail to print. 0.05 is a good press and 0.15 a small one.",
        "Shadow fill" => "The shadow tone that closes up to solid ink at each printing. 0.95 is a good press and 0.85 a small one.",
        "Source ink" => "Density of the solid ink on the page being copied. Lower closes up more of the shadows.",
        "Page size auto" => "Works out how large the picture is printed from the photo's pixels at the PPI below.",
        "PPI" => "Pixels per inch used to work out the printed size from the photo.",
        "Page size" => "Long edge of the picture on the page, in mm. The book's full-page pictures are about 200. The press softens detail by a fixed distance on the page, so a smaller picture looks softer.",
        "Screen" => "Halftone screen ruling, in lines per inch. A screen cell can't hold detail finer than itself, so a coarser screen gives a softer picture.",
        "Ink density" => "How dark solid black ink is on the paper. 1.20 is uncoated cream stock like the book's and 1.85 is coated paper, from the German offset printing standard.",
        "Dot gain" => "How much halftone dots spread on the press, darkening the middle tones, measured at a 50% dot. The press adds about 0.2 and most of it was allowed for when the plates were made.",
        "Smallest dot" => "Highlight dots smaller than this don't print, so the palest tones drop to bare paper. It has no visible effect in the Scan view, which shows those tones as white anyway.",
        "Camera blur" => "Softness of the camera that photographed the print for the press, in mm on the page.",
        "Paper scatter" => "Light spreading inside the paper before it reflects back, in mm. It softens dots and fine detail.",
        "View" => "How the printed page is shown. Auto and Scan set levels the way the book's reference scans were made. Ink on white and Ink on cream show the true ink on that paper. Print shows the darkroom print before the press.",
        "Scan black" => "The brightness the Scan view gives solid ink.",
        "Scan white" => "The page brightness the Scan view shows as white.",
        _ => return None,
    })
}
